To create a simple rate limiter middleware for an Express application in Node.js that allows 5 requests per second per IP using a sliding window algorithm, you can implement it as follows:

1. Store the timestamps of the requests made by each IP.
2. Check the number of requests in the last second for each IP.
3. Allow or deny the request based on the count.

Here’s a sample implementation of the rate limiter middleware:

### Step 1: Set up your Express app

First, ensure you have an Express app set up. If you don't have it yet, you can create a simple one:

```bash
mkdir express-rate-limiter
cd express-rate-limiter
npm init -y
npm install express
```

### Step 2: Create the rate limiter middleware

Now, create the middleware. You can create a new file called `rateLimiter.js`:

```javascript
// rateLimiter.js
const rateLimiter = (limit, windowMs) => {
    const requests = new Map();

    return (req, res, next) => {
        const ip = req.ip;
        const currentTime = Date.now();
        const timeWindow = currentTime - windowMs;

        // Initialize the request array if it doesn't exist
        if (!requests.has(ip)) {
            requests.set(ip, []);
        }

        const timestamps = requests.get(ip);

        // Remove timestamps that are outside the time window
        while (timestamps.length && timestamps[0] < timeWindow) {
            timestamps.shift();
        }

        // Check if the number of requests is within the limit
        if (timestamps.length < limit) {
            // Add the current timestamp
            timestamps.push(currentTime);
            requests.set(ip, timestamps);
            next(); // Allow the request to continue
        } else {
            // Limit exceeded
            res.status(429).send('Too many requests. Please try again later.');
        }
    };
};

// Usage
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Apply the rate limiter middleware
const requestLimit = 5; // Number of requests
const windowMs = 1000; // Time window in milliseconds (1 second)

app.use(rateLimiter(requestLimit, windowMs));

// Example route
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
    res.send('Hello, World!');
});

// Start the server
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
    console.log(`Server is running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```

